<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · Celebration · 1642 · Americas</p><h1 class="sg-title">Huron Carol</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="wdt">Jesous Ahatonhia</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>Canada's oldest Christmas song, written around 1642 by a French missionary in the language of the Wendat (Huron) people.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Jean de Brébeuf</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional (the French folk song &quot;Une Jeune Pucelle&quot;)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Huron-Wendat Christians and the Jesuit mission among them; later Canadian churches</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Wendat</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Sainte-Marie among the Hurons, Canada</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>probably 1642</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred · Celebration</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Jean de Brébeuf (c. 1642; he died in 1649))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>The &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huron-carol">Huron Carol</a>&quot; is a Christmas hymn, counted as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>'s oldest Christmas song. It was written, probably in 1642, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-de-brebeuf">Jean de Brébeuf</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> missionary at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sainte-marie-among-the-hurons">Sainte-Marie among the Hurons</a>, in the language of the Huron-Wendat people. Its Wendat title is &quot;Jesous Ahatonhia&quot;, &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>, he is born&quot;. The tune is that of a traditional French folk song, &quot;Une Jeune Pucelle&quot; (&quot;A Young Maid&quot;).</p><p>The carol remains a common Christmas hymn in Canadian churches of many denominations, and it stands in several American hymnals, among them The Hymnal 1982 of the Episcopal Church (No. 114), The United Methodist Hymnal (No. 244) and Evangelical Lutheran Worship (No. 284). Its tune keeps to a narrow range, which suits instruments of limited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnetic-compass">compass</a> such as the Native American flute.</p><p>The carol has been recorded many times, sometimes in the original Wendat: by Burl Ives in 1952 as &quot;Jesous Ahatonia&quot;, by Bruce Cockburn in Huron, and by Heather Dale in 2002 in Wendat, French and English.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A Christmas carol telling of the birth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> for a Wendat congregation, in their own language. Its tone is gentle and joyful, close to the folk song whose tune it borrows.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Wendat</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="wdt">Estenniayon de tsonwe Iesous ahatonnia</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="wdt">onn' awatewa nd' oki n' onyouandaskwaentak</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="wdt">ennonchien eskwatrihotat n'onyouandiyonrachatha</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="wdt">Iesous ahatonnia, ahatonnia. Iesous ahatonnia.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="wdt">Ayoki onkiennhache eronhiayeronnon</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="wdt">iontonk ontatiande ndio sen tsatonnharonnion</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="wdt">Warie onn' awakweton ndio sen tsatonnharonnion</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="wdt">Iesous ahatonnia, ahatonnia. Iesous ahatonnia.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="wdt">Achienhkontahonraskwa d' hatirihwannens</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="wdt">tichion sayonniondetha onhwa achia ahatren</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="wdt">ondaie te hahahakwa tichion sayonniondetha</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="wdt">Iesous ahatonnia, ahatonnia. Iesous ahatonnia.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="wdt">Tho ichien st' ahation tethotondi Iesous</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="wdt">ahwatatende tichion stanchitehawennion</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="wdt">asayontorenten ihatonk atsion sken</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="wdt">Iesous ahatonnia, ahatonnia. Iesous ahatonnia.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="wdt">Onne ontahation chiahonayen iesous</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="wdt">ahatichiennonniannon kahachia handiayon</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="wdt">te honannonronkwannion ihotonk werisen</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="wdt">Iesous ahatonnia, ahatonnia. Iesous ahatonnia.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="wdt">Te hekwatatennonten ahekwachiendaen</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="wdt">ti hekwannonronkwannion de sonywentenrände</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="wdt">outoyeti skwannonhwe icherhe akennonhonstha</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="wdt">Iesous ahatonnia, ahatonnia. Iesous ahatonnia.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Jean de Brébeuf (c. 1642); the original Wendat words, as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Huron_Carol" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="fnWZ7M2dLlM" data-title="Huron Carol: Heather Dale, in Wendat, French, English and American Sign Language"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">Heather Dale, in Wendat, French, English and American Sign Language</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel Heather Dale. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fnWZ7M2dLlM" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">'Twas in the Moon of Wintertime (Jesse Edgar Middleton)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1926</span><span class="sg-chain-h">English words, a new text rather than a close translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Christmas Day in the Morning (Burl Ives), also as the single &quot;Indian Christmas Carol&quot;</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1952</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording as &quot;Jesous Ahatonia&quot;</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Mystery of Christmas (Elora Festival Singers, arrangement by R. Anderson)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1997</span><span class="sg-chain-h">choral arrangement</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Midwinter (Terry McDade and The McDades)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2001</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording with Indian bansuri</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Jingle All the Way (Crash Test Dummies)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2002</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">This Endris Night (Heather Dale)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2002</span><span class="sg-chain-h">trilingual version (Wendat, French, English, the English translation attributed to Father H. Kierans); 2017 video with American Sign Language</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Canadian Tenors</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2011</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Wonderland (Sarah McLachlan)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2016</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Bruce Cockburn</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording in Huron</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Tom Jackson's Huron Carole tour</span><span class="sg-chain-h">performed annually</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Mi'kmaw translation by Mildred Milliea (sung by the Eskasoni Trio)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Lake Huron Carol</span><span class="sg-chain-h">rewritten rock version</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Une Jeune Pucelle</span><span class="sg-chain-h">French folk song; same melody</span></li></ul></section>
